Solution:
It may help to rewrite f(x) = [\sin (5 x)]^{6}. Use the Chain Rule with inside function u = \sin (5 x) and outside function y = u^{6}. A second application of Chain Rule is used to find \frac{du}{dx}.
\begin{array}{rcl} f'(x) \amp =\amp \displaystyle\frac{dy}{du} \cdot \frac{du}{dx}\\ \amp \amp \\ \amp =\amp (6 u^{5})(\cos(5 x) \cdot 5) \\ \amp =\amp 30 \sin^{5}(5 x)\cos(5 x) \end{array}
